  "content": {
    "id": "p9-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "One root of $x^2+px+q=0$ is three times the other. Show that $3p^2=16q$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$4\\alpha=-p$ and $3\\alpha^2=q$, so $3\\left(-\\frac{p}{4}\\right)^2=q$, giving $3p^2=16q$.",
      "worked": "Let the roots be $\\alpha$ and $3\\alpha$.\n\nSum of roots:\n$$4\\alpha=-p\\qquad(1)$$\nProduct of roots:\n$$3\\alpha^2=q\\qquad(2)$$\nRearrange (1): $\\alpha=-\\frac{p}{4}$. Substitute into (2):\n$$\\begin{aligned}3\\left(-\\frac{p}{4}\\right)^2&=q\\\\\\frac{3p^2}{16}&=q\\\\3p^2&=16q\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 42
  },
